      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Traditionally, the use of a nasogastric tube (NGT) after a laparotomy is said to prevent vomiting, aspiration, abdominal distension and paralytic ileus, which are likely to complicate the postoperative course."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine if discontinuation of NGT within 24 hours of abdominal surgical procedures in children has any effect on postoperative recovery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "We prospectively studied children who needed NGT passed for abdominal surgical procedures. NGTs were removed within 24 hours in all but 46 children who had the tube in situ for 3 to 5 days. Time to first and full oral feeds, length of hospital stay and complications were compared between the groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Children who had their NGTs removed within 24 hours (N = 120, Group 1) were compared with those who had NGT in place for 3 to 5 days (N = 46, Group 2). The mean time to first oral sips was 1.02 ± 0.13 days for Group 1 and 3.09 ± 0.29 days for Group 2 (p = 0.001). The mean time to full feeding was 2.22 ± 0.54 days for Group 1 and 4.54 ± 0.55 days for Group 2 (p = 0.001). Mean length of hospital stay (LOHS) was 8.32 ± 5.49 days for Group 1 and 12.78 ± 8.79 days for Group 2 (p = 0.001). Mean LOHS was 9.55 ± 6.85 days for both groups combined. Ten complications associated with the removal of the NGT occurred in both groups- 6 in Group 1 and 4 in Group 2 (p = 0.37). These were mainly vomiting and abdominal distension."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Our findings suggest that routine use of NGTs for decompression after laparotomy may be safely dispensed with after the child has recovered from anaesthesia."
        }
      ]},
